The cleanest is to end a chat by asking for a handoff block before you leave it — "give me a 5-line handoff I can paste to start the next chat: what we decided, what's still open, what file supersedes what." Then your next chat's first message is that block plus the relevant file. You did exactly this in your "FB Post Part 2" chat (started from a handoff file) and it worked.
The second is to reference the output file by name. Since your project files are always loaded, if the old chat saved eng_fix_masemaine_anchor_variety_260613.docx into the project, a new chat can just say "per the anchor-variety fix doc, now do X." I can read it.
The third, for cross-chat memory, is that I can search your past project chats — like I just did for this audit. So even without a handoff, saying "continue the protein-variety work from last week" lets me find it. But this is the most expensive option (it burns a search and may miss nuance), so prefer an explicit handoff or a named file.
One caveat worth knowing: searchable past chats are scoped to the project. A chat you started outside this project won't be found from inside it, and vice versa — which is another reason the co-founder/VC questions should live in their own space.
